{"duplicateCheck": {"label": "Duplicate tracking numbers found across all uploaded batches:", "count": 0, "message": "✓ No overlap/duplicate detected - every tracking number across your 7 SOA batches is unique. Digilex has not double-billed or double-counted any parcel."}, "explanationTitle": "WHAT \"PARCELS DELIVERED\" vs \"PARCELS FULFILLED\" ACTUALLY MEANS", "explanationText": "These two numbers come from two DIFFERENT counting windows inside the same SOA - they are not \"one is a subset of the other.\" Parcels Delivered = how many parcels had a completed COD collection recorded inside this batch's date range. Parcels Fulfilled = how many parcels Digilex physically packed and dispatched to the courier inside this batch's date range. A parcel dispatched near the end of one batch's window often does not get delivered (and therefore does not get counted as \"Delivered\") until the NEXT batch - and a parcel dispatched in an earlier batch can show up as \"Delivered\" in this batch. That is why Fulfilled can be higher OR lower than Delivered in the same batch (both happen in your data - see the table below), and it is why the two numbers will almost never match exactly even when nothing is wrong.\n\nYour example - Digilex-22-27.xlsx: Delivered = 189, Fulfilled = 240. This does NOT mean 51 parcels were returned-to-sender (RTS). It means 240 parcels were dispatched during May 22-27, while 189 parcels (a different, overlapping group - some dispatched earlier, some in this window) were confirmed delivered and paid out during May 22-27. Digilex's export has no separate \"confirmed RTS\" field, so a dispatched parcel with no matching delivery record yet could simply still be in transit, or could later be uploaded as delivered in a future batch. Orders Database now labels every such order \"Pending / Unmatched\" (yellow, not alarming) instead of assuming RTS - only change a row to \"Confirmed RTS\" once you've verified it directly with Digilex or the courier.", "batchTable": 